README.md
    1→Anyhow&ensp;¯\\\_(°ペ)\_/¯
    2→==========================
    4→[<img alt="github" src="https://img.shields.io/badge/github-dtolnay/anyhow-8da0cb?style=for-the-badge&labelColor=555555&logo=github" height="20">](https://github.com/dtolnay/anyhow)
    5→[<img alt="crates.io" src="https://img.shields.io/crates/v/anyhow.svg?style=for-the-badge&color=fc8d62&logo=rust" height="20">](https://crates.io/crates/anyhow)
    6→[<img alt="docs.rs" src="https://img.shields.io/badge/docs.rs-anyhow-66c2a5?style=for-the-badge&labelColor=555555&logo=docs.rs" height="20">](https://docs.rs/anyhow)
    7→[<img alt="build status" src="https://img.shields.io/github/actions/workflow/status/dtolnay/anyhow/ci.yml?branch=master&style=for-the-badge" height="20">](https://github.com/dtolnay/anyhow/actions?query=branch%3Amaster)
    9→This library provides [`anyhow::Error`][Error], a trait object based error type
    10→for easy idiomatic error handling in Rust applications.
    12→[Error]: https://docs.rs/anyhow/1.0/anyhow/struct.Error.html
    14→```toml
    15→[dependencies]
    16→anyhow = "1.0"
    17→```
    19→<br>
    126→## No-std support
    128→In no_std mode, almost all of the same API is available and works the same way.
    129→To depend on Anyhow in no_std mode, disable our default enabled "std" feature in
    130→Cargo.toml. A global allocator is required.
    132→```toml
    133→[dependencies]
    134→anyhow = { version = "1.0", default-features = false }
    135→```
    137→With versions of Rust older than 1.81, no_std mode may require an additional
    138→`.map_err(Error::msg)` when working with a non-Anyhow error type inside a
    139→function that returns Anyhow's error type, as the trait that `?`-based error
    140→conversions are defined by is only available in std in those old versions.
    142→<br>
    144→## Comparison to failure
    146→The `anyhow::Error` type works something like `failure::Error`, but unlike
    147→failure ours is built around the standard library's `std::error::Error` trait
    148→rather than a separate trait `failure::Fail`. The standard library has adopted
    149→the necessary improvements for this to be possible as part of [RFC 2504].
    151→[RFC 2504]: https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/blob/master/text/2504-fix-error.md
    153→<br>
    155→## Comparison to thiserror
    157→Use Anyhow if you don't care what error type your functions return, you just
    158→want it to be easy. This is common in application code. Use [thiserror] if you
    159→are a library that wants to design your own dedicated error type(s) so that on
    160→failures the caller gets exactly the information that you choose.
    162→[thiserror]: https://github.com/dtolnay/thiserror
    164→<br>
    166→#### License
    168→<sup>
    169→Licensed under either of <a href="LICENSE-APACHE">Apache License, Version
    170→2.0</a> or <a href="LICENSE-MIT">MIT license</a> at your option.
    171→</sup>
    173→<br>
    175→<sub>
    176→Unless you explicitly state otherwise, any contribution intentionally submitted
    177→for inclusion in this crate by you, as defined in the Apache-2.0 license, shall
    178→be dual licensed as above, without any additional terms or conditions.
    179→</sub>
